cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Coca Is Not Cocaine: Three Lexical-Cultural Collision Modes in
  Open-Weight LLMs, Probed in Catalan
message: If you use this dataset or code, please cite as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Vinaixa Roselló
    given-names: Xavier
    email: xavi@sorensen.ai
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-2769-9215"
    affiliation: "Sorensen AI (sorensen.ai), Barcelona, Catalonia"
    website: "https://sorensen.ai"
repository-code: "https://github.com/xaviviro/coca-is-not-cocaine"
url: "https://github.com/xaviviro/coca-is-not-cocaine"
abstract: >-
  Three-mode taxonomy of lexical-cultural collisions
  (cross_lingual_bias / domain_specific / internal_polysemy) for
  probing culturally-marked low-resource vocabulary in multilingual
  base LLMs, instantiated on a 56-concept Catalan lexicon and applied
  across eleven open-weight base models (Google Gemma, Alibaba Qwen,
  Mistral, DeepSeek, and BSC-LT Salamandra/ALIA — Catalan-aware
  control). Includes a five-method statistical-rigour suite
  (paired-bootstrap delta, layer-depth sweep, sub-clustered Anglo
  anchors, stratified-bootstrap CIs, Procrustes cross-model alignment),
  a pre-registered title test (n_noise ≥ 2 falsifier), and the empirical
  finding that intentional Catalan training does not erase the F1/F2/F3
  collisions — only Catalan-language framing at inference time does.
keywords:
  - cultural-bias
  - low-resource-language
  - catalan
  - llm-probing
